WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.823
Speaker 1: Let's hear from people in the audience.&nbsp;
We take calls via discord at David Pakman dot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.823 --> 00:00:15.680
com slash discord. You can not only engage in&nbsp;
moderated, polite discussion about whatever is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:23.280
important to you on the discord, it's also how we&nbsp;
take calls. Let's start today with Brad from New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.320
York. Brad, welcome. What's on your mind today?
Speaker 2: Hey, David. Thanks for taking my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:34.800
call. My pleasure. I think what I wanted&nbsp;
to discuss was I've been seeing a worrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:41.840
trend in that I am pretty far left and I like to&nbsp;
debate conservatives. And something I'm noticing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:50.640
nowadays is people are willing to be openly racist&nbsp;
or openly bigoted to get their points across.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:56.720
And I'll give a very quick example. I was&nbsp;
debating somebody. I said illegal immigration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.240
is not an issue. Very vague, very open. And&nbsp;
he said the only way to save America is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:07.680
prevent the white race from being bred out.&nbsp;
Right. And I've never encountered somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:12.720
just being blatantly racist like that. I have no&nbsp;
idea how to how to respond to that or if they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.520
even worth engaging with. What would you suggest?
Speaker 1: My instinct is that they're not worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.320
engaging with. But if you wanted, what I would&nbsp;
find really interesting is to figure out where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.000
they got those ideas. And I would start&nbsp;
by saying, Oh, that's really interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.760
So what makes you say that? What are the&nbsp;
differences you, you believe exist between white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.120
and nonwhite people? Like really drilled down to&nbsp;
exactly what the origins of their beliefs are?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:44.160
When did it first occur to you that white&nbsp;
people are superior to nonwhite people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.200
the ways that you're describing? I would&nbsp;
find it very interesting to really get at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.040
where those beliefs came from. But in terms&nbsp;
of changing those people's minds, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.520
I think it would be great if we could do it,&nbsp;
but I don't know that I would necessarily put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:01.200
too much time into it if I were you.
Speaker 2: Yeah, my emphasis isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.240
changing that person's mind. It's more of the&nbsp;
people that are listening to the debate have of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.440
picking their ideas apart. And it just, I guess,&nbsp;
becomes more difficult because you can't really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.480
logically dissect something that's rooted in&nbsp;
logic, theory or really an illogic very easily.&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.120
Speaker 1: Right. And I think what&nbsp;
you're getting at is you can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.920
help people reason their way out of positions&nbsp;
they didn't reason their way into to begin with.&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.080
Speaker 2: Exactly.
Speaker 1: Yeah. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:31.120
I would really out of in order to I think the&nbsp;
most useful thing in that type of situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.600
is to I don't mean generally I mean really&nbsp;
specifically get to why they believe those things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:42.560
because sometimes they might say, well, you know,&nbsp;
my dad is the one who originally instructed me on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.880
this. And then I would say, Really? Was your dad&nbsp;
ever wrong about anything? You know, I think it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.320
would open up an interesting conversation&nbsp;
to learn from. But like you're saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.760
not necessarily to change that person's mind.
Speaker 2: Okay. Yeah, because typically what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.520
I'll do is I'll ask for evidence because&nbsp;
I believe in data and they'll say to all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:04.640
1352 or whatever other white supremacist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.480
talking points they have an offhand yeah.
Speaker 1: Often they don't even understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.360
their own talking point. So I also&nbsp;
think it's worth getting them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.720
explain their views for that reason.
Speaker 2: Okay. Thank you. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.880
really helpful. I appreciate it.
Speaker 1: My pleasure. Thank you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:23.440
so much for the call. Yeah. That those those&nbsp;
conversations are both incredibly frustrating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:30.720
but also can be really illuminating if you sort&nbsp;
of are prepared and know and know what to ask.

